Subject: SSO: generalize the minting endpoint by audience — exocortexia joins mutable

Public items: 2 added, 0 removed, 2 changed. Dependencies did not change. Code lines after this commit: 15028.

commit 8660b05374d5982809b5f8831d699677d3214990
parent c65f4d86a01b9b7effd89dda0805f2e8fc80af03
author Emise <emise@localhost>
date 1794110774 2026-11-08
item~ function src/oauth.rs :: sso :: pub fn sso( &self, aud: &str, headers: &HashMap<String, String>, query: &HashMap<String, String>, ) -> Reply { :: `GET /api/auth/sso/<aud>` — the cross-site handoff to one allowlisted audience (`mutable`, `exocortexia`).  Layering follows the claims/submissions precedent in the servers: a signed-in account is enough; an editor's place is not required (`sso_admits` is the single predicate that decides). With a session, the reply 302s to the audience's callback with a freshly sealed assertion. Without one, it 302s to this site's own root with this endpoint as the `next`, so the ordinary provider sign-in returns the browser here and the handoff completes — the OAuth `next` itself stays same-site throughout. An audience outside the allowlist, or one not configured (no SSO secret, or no target for that audience), simply does not exist: 404 — checked before any session handling, so an unknown audience never enters the sign-in return flow. :: doc comment
item~ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_assertion :: pub fn sso_assertion( &self, aud: &str, session: &Session, editor: bool, ret: &str, ) -> Result<String, String> { :: Mint the assertion an audience's callback verifies: who this session is, whether it is an editor, and the return path on the audience's site — sealed under the shared SSO secret, alive for `SSO_TTL` seconds, with a fresh `jti` the audience burns on first sight. The format is identical for every audience; only the `aud` claim differs, and an audience outside the allowlist in `sso_target_for` can never be minted for. The return path is clamped by `safe_next` here, at the moment of sealing, so no unsigned return input ever crosses. :: doc comment
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_ready_for :: pub fn sso_ready_for(&self, aud: &str) -> bool { :: True when the SSO handoff is configured for one audience: the shared SSO secret is real and that audience has a target. Every allowlisted audience rides the same secret and the same sealed assertion format; only the `aud` claim and the callback origin differ. :: doc comment
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_target_for :: pub fn sso_target_for(&self, aud: &str) -> Option<&str> { :: The callback origin for an audience, or None when the audience is not allowlisted or is allowlisted but unconfigured. Mutable always has a target (its production origin is the default); Exocortexia has one only once `PHEXT_SSO_EXOCORTEXIA_URL` is set. This match is the audience allowlist — an audience that is not named here can never be minted for. :: doc comment
