Subject: SSO: desktop audience — loopback mint and server-side exchange for Phext Notepad

Public items: 7 added, 0 removed, 1 changed. Dependencies did not change. Code lines after this commit: 15722.

commit 60f1754acb93889ed6c8b1baefcb6428dc1bf1ac
parent 3c2e951d6df47cf4a9b8b5d484ce137705734bfe
author Emise <emise@mirrorborn.us>
date 1794123209 2026-11-08
item+ struct src/oauth.rs :: SsoAssertionBody :: pub struct SsoAssertionBody { :: The SSO assertion: a sealed, single-use statement that an edit session exists, carried to an audience's callback in a redirect and verified there under the SSO secret. It is not a session — the audience mints its own from it, and this server never accepts one back. `aud` names the one audience it was minted for (`mutable`, `exocortexia`, `wishnode`); a consumer must reject any other value, including an assertion minted for a different audience. `ret` is a path on the audience's site, clamped by `safe_next` before sealing; `jti` is the single-use id the audience burns on first sight.  The one exception to "never accepts one back" is the `desktop` audience (Phext Notepad): a shipped app cannot hold the SSO secret, so its assertions come back here to `POST /api/auth/desktop/exchange`, which verifies them under the secret and burns the `jti` server-side. For that audience `ret` is the app's validated loopback return URL (see `loopback_return`), and `st` carries the app's per-attempt state nonce, sealed at mint and demanded back at the exchange. Web audiences never carry `st` — the field is simply absent from their assertions. :: doc comment
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: issue_session :: pub fn issue_session( &self, who: &str, handle: &str, name: &str, email: &str, ) -> Result<(String, String), String> { :: Seal a fresh session for an identity that has already been established — by the provider exchange in `finish`, or by a verified desktop SSO assertion at the exchange endpoint. This is the one place session values are made: a fresh CSRF token and a `SESSION_TTL` seal under the session secret. Returns the sealed cookie value and the CSRF token inside it (the desktop exchange hands both to the app; the browser flow learns the CSRF from `/api/session` instead). :: doc comment
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: loopback_return :: pub fn loopback_return(raw: &str) -> Option<String> { :: Admit a desktop return URL: the address of the listener the app opened for this one sign-in attempt. The bar is the loopback literal itself, checked character by character — not a URL parse that a hostname or userinfo could slip through:  * scheme `http` exactly. Loopback has a real origin but no CA issues certificates for it; RFC 8252's native-app practice is plain http on the loopback interface, which never leaves the machine. * host the literal `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]` — not `localhost`, not any other name, not another loopback address. A name is whatever the resolver says it is (hosts files are editable), and the app binds the numeric loopback anyway. * an explicit port, 1–65535 — the app always listens on an ephemeral port it was given, never a default. * then only a path/query tail of plain URL characters: no `#` (a fragment never reaches a server anyway, and its presence means the value was not a listener address), no whitespace, controls, or non-ASCII.  The result is capped short enough to ride the sign-in bounce — this endpoint travels as the `next` of a `/?next=` redirect, and `safe_next` refuses anything over 200 characters there. :: doc comment
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: open_sso_assertion :: pub fn open_sso_assertion(&self, token: &str) -> Result<SsoAssertionBody, String> { :: Open an SSO assertion under the SSO secret — the exchange endpoint is the one place this server ever takes an assertion back, and only the desktop audience's. Expiry is checked here; audience, state, and single-use are the caller's to enforce (the burn lives on the `Gate`, which owns request state). Every failure is the same error: the exchange must not say which check a forged assertion failed. :: doc comment
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_assertion_desktop :: pub fn sso_assertion_desktop( &self, session: &Session, editor: bool, ret: &str, state: &str, ) -> Result<String, String> { :: Mint the desktop audience's assertion. Same sealed format as every audience, with the two differences that audience exists for: `ret` is the app's loopback return URL, validated by `loopback_return` here at the moment of sealing (anything else is refused, so no unvalidated return ever crosses), and the app's per-attempt `state` nonce rides sealed as `st`, to be demanded back at the exchange — an assertion that arrives anywhere without its attempt's state is not that attempt's assertion. The caller (`Gate::sso_desktop`) has already validated the state's shape. :: doc comment
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_desktop_ready :: pub fn sso_desktop_ready(&self) -> bool { :: True when the desktop audience is armed: a real SSO secret and the operator's explicit `PHEXT_SSO_DESKTOP=1`. There is no target to consult — the app's callback is a per-attempt loopback listener — so readiness is the secret plus the flag. :: doc comment
item~ function src/oauth.rs :: sso_target_for :: pub fn sso_target_for(&self, aud: &str) -> Option<&str> { :: The callback origin for an audience, or None when the audience is not allowlisted or is allowlisted but unconfigured. Mutable always has a target (its production origin is the default); Exocortexia has one only once `PHEXT_SSO_EXOCORTEXIA_URL` is set, and Wishnode only once `PHEXT_SSO_WISHNODE_URL` is. This match is the audience allowlist — an audience that is not named here can never be minted for. The desktop audience is deliberately absent: it has no fixed origin (its callback is the app's per-attempt loopback listener), so it is armed and minted on its own path (`sso_desktop_ready`, `sso_assertion_desktop`). :: doc comment
item+ function src/oauth.rs :: valid_state_nonce :: pub fn valid_state_nonce(state: &str) -> bool { :: The desktop attempt's state nonce: long enough to be unguessable, plain enough to travel in URLs and assertions unchanged. The app generates it (32 random hex/letters in practice); this only checks the shape. :: doc comment
