Commit 3e3beb35f904 by Theia <theia@mirrorborn.us> on 2026-11-10 (EE 0).

Subject: oauth: let authorize requests survive the sign-in return

Public items: 0 added, 0 removed, 2 changed. Dependencies did not change. Code lines after this commit: 26764.

commit 3e3beb35f9049ae99f34ee7a2b7685a8c265eb0e
parent 797d3157089803a355d7467d855c7f2cb4f3c256
author Theia <theia@mirrorborn.us>
date 1794283244 2026-11-10
item~ function src/oauth.rs :: loopback_return :: pub fn loopback_return(raw: &str) -> Option<String> { :: Admit a desktop return URL: the address of the listener the app opened for this one sign-in attempt. The bar is the loopback literal itself, checked character by character — not a URL parse that a hostname or userinfo could slip through:  * scheme `http` exactly. Loopback has a real origin but no CA issues certificates for it; RFC 8252's native-app practice is plain http on the loopback interface, which never leaves the machine. * host the literal `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]` — not `localhost`, not any other name, not another loopback address. A name is whatever the resolver says it is (hosts files are editable), and the app binds the numeric loopback anyway. * an explicit port, 1–65535 — the app always listens on an ephemeral port it was given, never a default. * then only a path/query tail of plain URL characters: no `#` (a fragment never reaches a server anyway, and its presence means the value was not a listener address), no whitespace, controls, or non-ASCII.  The result is capped short enough to ride the sign-in bounce — this endpoint travels as the `next` of a `/?next=` redirect, well inside the length `safe_next` admits there. :: doc comment
item~ function src/oauth.rs :: safe_next :: pub fn safe_next(next: &str) -> String { :: Keeps the post-sign-in redirect on this site: a path, optionally with a `#coordinate` fragment.  A `:` is refused in the path portion only. Past the `?`, a query may legitimately carry URLs of its own — an OAuth authorization request returning from sign-in names https redirect URIs and, for CIMD clients, an https client_id — and a colon there cannot move the destination off this site, because the value still begins with a single `/` and browsers resolve it as a path on this origin. The length cap admits those same authorization requests (a full `/oauth/authorize?...` round trip runs several hundred characters); the sealed pending cookie that carries `next` through a provider sign-in holds it comfortably. :: doc comment
